Correction: the demonstration dash did not read as a dash

Raised by the operator on seeing the rendered animation.

Three faults, all in the numbers rather than the engine:
  duration   most of a second, about three times too long for a dash
  distance   one body width, too short for anything to read as fast
  spacing    eleven frames across that distance, a few pixels each, which
             reads as sliding

Underneath them, the sample character's canvas was one body wide, so a dash
could not be watched on it at all - the character simply left the picture.

A fourth fault surfaced while fixing those: the renderer widened the canvas by
the travel distance measured from the edge of the cel, not the edge of the
drawing. A cel covering the whole canvas made those wildly different, and the
output was mostly empty space.

Now: six frames, four times the distance, about a third of a second, and a
gentler deceleration so the closing frames still travel instead of sitting
almost still. The sample has room to move into, and the renderer widens only
when the motion would genuinely run off.

Verified by rendering in batch and looking at every frame of the tagged range
in order. The interactive check could not be repeated: synthetic input stopped
reaching the editor partway through the session, with the window still reported
as active. The tool documentation now says so rather than claiming otherwise.

